    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mammalian cells require the presence of growth factors in order to enter the cell cycle and divide. However, constant exposure to growth factors is not always required for cells to divide in tissue culture. In fact, giving cells two short pulses of growth factors eight hours apart is just as effective as giving cells constant growth factors in driving them into the cell cycle. This suggests that there are two time points within the G1 phase of the cell cycle during which growth factor stimulation is required. The first, competence, prepares the cell to respond to the second, progression. Progression causes the cell to continue through the restriction point and into the cell cycle. Whether competence and progression are present in primary human lung fibroblasts remains unclear. To characterize the presence of competence and progression in primary mammalian cells, we released primary human lung fibroblasts from serum starvation and measured cell cycle phase and cell size changes over time. In both constant and pulsed growth factor conditions cell cycle progression and cell growth were similar.  Furthermore, we show that there is very little cell growth in the period of G1 between competence and progression. This suggests that providing a cell with a competence factor is not sufficient to induce the cell growth required for G1 exit.",
